4	
5	    Add project skills and fire the model-arrived skill on every in-app arrival
6	    
7	    A project skill is a file on the Agent page, AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md:
8	    frontmatter with its name and a trigger, a description or both, then the
9	    text the model gets. The page sends every skill once per connection in one
10	    skills message, which replaces file_report_instructions. The host refuses a
11	    bad payload with a bridge error and keeps the set it had; names are sorted,
12	    one skill per trigger, 16 KiB of text each and 256 KiB in all. Bridge version
13	    stays 1; the capability list gains project_skills. Project turns the app
14	    starts before the skills arrive wait for them, as they wait for the project
15	    instructions.
16	    
17	    A skill with a trigger is the app's: when its event happens, the host adds it
18	    as the last item of the one request where the model first reads about the
19	    event, after a picture of the active plate (the offscreen iso render
20	    view_render uses, drawn when the request is built and only when the plate
21	    has a printable object). Neither is stored in the conversation, and
22	    OpenAIResponsesAgent leaves both out of the history the turn's next request
23	    is built from. A skill with a description is the model's: skill_read, ported
24	    from the suspended skills branch, lists the described skills in its own
25	    description and reads only those, and is offered only while one exists. None
26	    does yet.
27	    
28	    The first skill, model-opened-or-imported (trigger model_arrived), is the
29	    former fileReportInstructions.ts text. It fires when the person opens or
30	    drops a file; after an in-app object_import, or a project_open that fails, in
31	    the same turn; and after an in-app project_open that succeeds. That open
32	    replaces the conversation with the opened project's and ends the turn that
33	    asked, so the coordinator hands its load report to the host, which posts it
34	    in the opened project's chat on the next timer tick through the path a
35	    person's open takes: the file note, then a turn with the skill and the
36	    picture. A reopened project with its own conversation is greeted only when
37	    OrcaSlicer said something while loading it, as before; continuing an earlier
38	    project loads nothing and says nothing. MCP clients' loads fire nothing.
39	    
40	    The text now covers both ways a model arrives; tells the model to look at the
41	    attached picture and guess what the object is, what it is for and how to
42	    slice it; to check the selected presets and plate type against that guess and
43	    the presets available and suggest a better one without changing it; and to
44	    end with its best guess at what the person wants next, in whatever form fits.
45	    It is 6,918 bytes, from 6,635. When the chat has no setup title and no
46	    answered intent field, the host appends one fixed sentence saying nothing is
47	    recorded about what the print is for. view_render's description also offers
48	    the picture for telling what an object is; project_tools.json is regenerated.
49	    
50	    The project prompt eval gains three first-step cases that start at the
51	    request carrying the skill: model-opened-unnamed (the person imports a model
52	    whose name says nothing about it), model-imported-by-tool (object_import of
53	    an attached model) and model-opened-by-tool (project_open, in the opened
54	    project's chat). The recorder checks the skill and picture are on exactly
55	    that request and absent from the next, and stores how many items were sent
56	    once; the runner drops those before it answers a read. requests.json is
57	    recorded again; the earlier points are unchanged apart from ids, timestamps
58	    and temporary paths. No live batch has been run.
